dialect protocol
protocol stdio.pdf.snapshot
version 1
schema stdio.pdf
start header

# P2-FG3: `stdio.pdf` (1.4) pack payload is the REAL raw PDF file bytes (`store::ArtifactPack::
# encode_pack_with`, ../../../../../⚙️engine's `PdfSnapshot` schema module -> real `encode_pdf(self)`,
# wrapped verbatim by the SEMIO binary envelope -- this file models the bytes as if the walk
# already starts AT the payload, past the envelope, per M3's own guidance).
#
# `framing record` (NOT `framing magic ...`) is deliberate, confirmed live by a real
# `walk_protocol` run, not assumed: `Framing::Magic` unconditionally reads/compares EXACTLY 8
# raw bytes regardless of how many hex digits the declared literal has
# (`🗣️dsl/📖️grammar/🦀️component.rs`'s own `magic_bytes(value: u64) -> [u8; 8]`) -- PDF's real
# magic is only 4 bytes (`%PDF`), so `framing magic 0x25504446` gets read as an 8-byte,
# left-zero-padded `[0,0,0,0,0x25,0x50,0x44,0x46]` and mismatches the real on-disk bytes (a
# genuine dialect gap, `protocol-framing-magic-fixed-8-bytes`, first hit by gif87a's own
# 6-byte signature). Modeled honestly, same fix gif87a's own protocol file documents: a real,
# individually-read `field magic fixed 4` inside a `header` block -- genuinely walked but NOT
# byte-validated at the PROTOCOL layer (the real Rust `decode_pdf` already validates it
# byte-for-byte, `data.starts_with(b"%PDF")`, ⚙️engine/component.rs:1236 [1.7's shared decoder,
# which 1.4's own frozen-stub `decode_pdf` mirrors with its own local 4-byte check]).
framing record

header fixed 4
field magic fixed 4

# 1.4's own real `decode_pdf` (../../../../../⚙️engine/🦀️component.rs) checks only the 4-byte
# `%PDF` magic then does a raw FORWARD byte-search for `stream`/`endstream` -- no xref/trailer/
# object-graph parsing exists at all (the "1.4 stays a frozen stub" scope boundary, W0 recon +
# this ticket's own binding note: "keep it minimally alive under its own path -- do NOT give it
# the full 1.7 object-graph model"). No backward-scan/xref structure to model here (unlike 1.7's
# own protocol file) -- past the real magic, the rest is one opaque trailing chain, the honest
# boundary for a linear substring-search codec with no declarative block structure of its own.
chain payload bytes
